    cacrep: 'human_growth',
    topic: 'developmental life transitions',
    difficulty: 'medium',
    stem: `An 18-year-old starting college has planned since childhood to become a pharmacist like both of his parents. He has never explored other paths or values and sees no reason to. Which of Marcia's identity statuses BEST describes him?`,
    options: [
      { id: 'a', text: `Foreclosure`, isCorrect: true, rationale: `Commitment to a path chosen by others, without any exploration, defines foreclosure.` },
      { id: 'b', text: `Moratorium`, isCorrect: false, rationale: `Moratorium involves active exploration before commitment, which he has not done.` },
      { id: 'c', text: `Diffusion`, isCorrect: false, rationale: `Diffusion involves neither exploration nor commitment, but he is firmly committed.` },
      { id: 'd', text: `Achievement`, isCorrect: false, rationale: `Achievement follows a period of exploration, which he has skipped.` },
    ],
    rationale: `Marcia defined four identity statuses by exploration and commitment: achievement (explored, then committed), moratorium (exploring, not yet committed), foreclosure (committed without exploring) and diffusion (neither). Foreclosed young people often adopt parents' plans and can struggle when the transition to college or work challenges those plans. Counselors can invite gentle exploration without dismissing the client's commitment.`,
    references: [{ source: 'Berk (Development Through the Lifespan)', detail: 'Adolescence: Marcia\'s identity statuses (achievement, moratorium, foreclosure, diffusion)' }],
